  "body": "## The Guide was named after the software\n\nSections were called Models, Analytics, Key rotation, Security. Someone opening a guide is **stuck on a task**, not curious about a feature area. The headings now say what you are trying to do:\n\n| Was | Now |\n| --- | --- |\n| Models | Map model tiers |\n| Claude Code (CLI) | Point Claude Code here |\n| Analytics | See what a request actually did |\n| Key rotation | Use more than one key safely |\n| Web search | Search the web |\n| Security | Lock it down |\n\nContent changing shape to match how it is read, rather than a visual layer on top of it.\n\n**Every `id=\"guide-...\"` is byte-identical.** Deep links, the scrollspy and the Guide's own cross-references key off those — renaming a heading is a copy edit; renaming its id breaks every link anyone saved.\n\n## Three cross-links, a copy button\n\nThree links, each making a connection the prose already states clickable. This needed a `.guide-body a` rule, because there was none — prose links would otherwise have rendered in **default browser blue**, precisely the kind of unplanned hue a fixed palette exists to prevent.\n\nCode blocks get a copy button. They hold env vars, a `settings.json` snippet and shell commands a reader is going to paste. `navigator.clipboard` is **feature-detected rather than assumed**, so a browser that withholds it never renders the button instead of rendering a broken one, and the write promise fails silently — the text stays selectable either way.\n\n## Get Started splits by necessity\n\nThree required steps form a genuine causal chain: a provider, then a model, then pointing the client at it. The four optional ones are independent extras. Rendering all seven as one list with a per-card pill as the only difference meant **reading every pill to learn which three actually matter**.\n\nThe split is derived from `step.optional` while walking the already-ordered array — no reorder, no new field, nothing persisted, and the expand/advance state machine is untouched. It also makes the progress bar honest: \"N of M essential steps done\" now describes the group directly above it.\n\nA step that is **done and collapsed** shows a check instead of a chevron, so scanning the closed list distinguishes \"closed because handled\" from \"closed because I have not opened it yet\" without reading text. Deliberately nothing more — no numerals, no ring, no celebration. This is someone configuring a proxy.\n\n## One trade-off, stated rather than hidden\n\nThe rail is a 224px column; after padding, ~180px, or roughly 25 characters at 13px. \"Point Claude Desktop here\" is 26. Rather than gamble on wrap behaviour that cannot be rendered and checked here, the three \"Point X here\" entries were shortened to plain app names **in the TOC** while keeping the fuller task phrasing on the heading, where width is not a constraint.\n\nThat is a real retreat from task-framing for those three, bought with certainty.\n\n## Rejected, recorded so they are not re-proposed\n\n- **In-page search** — duplicates Ctrl+F, and a single scrolling column was chosen precisely to keep find-on-page and `#anchor` links working.\n- **A density pass** — a stressed reader wants topics separated, not crammed.\n- **Step numerals** — order is already carried by the grouping and position.\n\n## Verification\n\n```\n$ uv run ty check\nAll checks passed!\n\n$ env -u ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS -u EXA_API_KEY uv run pytest tests/api tests/config -q\n926 passed in 45.83s\n```\n\nThe class guard added in 4.35.2 **caught a class added during this work with no matching rule** — removed rather than given an unused selector. Anchor ids and the onboarding state machine confirmed unchanged by diffing for them specifically. `node --check` clean.\n\nThe `.fallback-*` block restored on this branch merged with the copy already on main **without duplicating a single rule** — checked explicitly, since two definitions of one selector was the obvious hazard.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **No browser**, so the copy button's position over short single-line code blocks, the shortened TOC entries' wrap behaviour, and the group divider's spacing are arithmetic against the box model, not rendered measurements.\n- The Guide's bundled screenshots date to 4.24.0 and are now further behind after this week's Model Config and Web Search redesigns. Unchanged here; recapturing needs a browser and a server with real traffic.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
