    "message": "feat(admin): frame the Guide around tasks and split Get Started by necessity\n\nThe Guide's sections were named after the software -- Models, Analytics, Key\nrotation, Security. Someone opening a guide is stuck on a task, not curious\nabout a feature area, so the headings now say what you are trying to do:\n\"Map model tiers\", \"See what a request actually did\", \"Use more than one key\nsafely\", \"Lock it down\". Content changing shape to match how it is read,\nrather than a visual layer on top of it.\n\nEvery id=\"guide-...\" is byte-identical. Deep links, the scrollspy and the\nGuide's own cross-references key off those; renaming a heading is a copy\nedit, renaming its id breaks every link anyone saved.\n\nThree cross-links added, each one making a connection the prose already\nstates clickable: the 404 row's \"model id\", and two troubleshooting entries.\nThis needed a .guide-body a rule, because there was none -- prose links would\notherwise have rendered in default browser blue, which is precisely the kind\nof unplanned hue a fixed palette exists to prevent.\n\nCode blocks get a copy button. These blocks hold env vars, a settings.json\nsnippet and shell commands that a reader is going to paste, and retyping them\nis the friction the page exists to remove. navigator.clipboard is\nfeature-detected rather than assumed, so a browser that withholds it never\nrenders the button instead of rendering a broken one, and the write promise\nfails silently -- the text stays selectable either way.\n\nGet Started renders in two groups. Three required steps form a genuine causal\nchain (a provider, then a model, then pointing the client at it); the four\noptional ones are independent extras. Rendering all seven as one list, with a\nper-card pill as the only difference, meant reading every pill to learn which\nthree actually matter. The split is derived from step.optional while walking\nthe already-ordered array -- no reorder, no new field, nothing persisted, and\nthe expand/advance state machine is untouched. It also makes the progress bar\nhonest: \"N of M essential steps done\" now describes the group directly above\nit.\n\nA step that is done and collapsed shows a check instead of a chevron, so a\nscan of the closed list distinguishes \"closed because handled\" from \"closed\nbecause I have not opened it yet\" without reading any text. Deliberately\nnothing more: no numerals, no ring, no celebration. This is someone\nconfiguring a proxy.\n\nOne trade-off worth stating rather than hiding. The rail is a 224px column;\nafter padding, about 180px, or roughly 25 characters at 13px. \"Point Claude\nDesktop here\" is 26. Rather than gamble on wrap behaviour that cannot be\nrendered and checked here, the three \"Point X here\" entries were shortened to\nplain app names in the TOC while keeping the fuller task phrasing on the\nheading, where width is not a constraint. That is a real retreat from\ntask-framing for those three, bought with certainty.\n\nRejected, and worth recording so they are not re-proposed: in-page search\n(duplicates Ctrl+F, and a scrolling column was chosen precisely to keep\nfind-on-page and #anchor links working), a density pass (a stressed reader\nwants topics separated, not crammed), and step numerals (order is already\ncarried by the grouping and position).\n\nVerified: 926 tests pass, including the class guard added in 4.35.2, which\ncaught a class added during this work with no matching rule -- removed rather\nthan given an unused selector. ruff, ty and node --check clean. Anchor ids and\nthe onboarding state machine confirmed unchanged by diffing for them\nspecifically. The failover CSS restored on this branch merged with the copy\nalready on main without duplicating a single rule; checked explicitly.\n\nNot verified: no browser, so the copy button's position over short code\nblocks, the shortened TOC entries' wrap behaviour, and the group divider's\nspacing are arithmetic against the box model, not rendered measurements.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
